  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186054676,
      "id": "3123f8ed-a1ca-52b7-8427-2f3b60d9e68a",
      "title": "Identifying Points That Lie on a Line"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "e8c73548-70f1-5166-8246-f3e2d1c9e837",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/repairs/2026-10-19-checkbox-nonempty/baseline-samples/17592186054676/assets/b705f4ffdb8a9fbe4e1ada20c7e90445b9b05f0aeb54ca2ade8b9a5b8868c6b4.png)\n\nIdentify the points that lie on the line $y=2x$.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The plotted points correspond to the $xy$-pairs $A(-3,-6)$, $B(1,5)$, $C(0,0)$.\n\nTo check whether a point lies on the line, we substitute the coordinates of the point into the equation and determine whether the resulting statement is true.\n\n- Substituting the point $A(-3,-6)$ results in a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &= 2x \\\\ (-6) &= 2(-3) \\\\ -6 &= -6 && \\checkmark\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $A$ does lie on the line.\n\n- Substituting the point $B(1,5)$ results in a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &= 2x \\\\ (5) &= 2(1) \\\\ 5 &= 2 && \\times\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $B$ does not lie on the line.\n\n- Substituting the point $C(0,0)$ results in a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &= 2x \\\\ (0) &= 2(0) \\\\ 0 &= 0 && \\checkmark\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $C$ does lie on the line.\n\nFinally, only points $A$ and $C$ lie on the line.",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "7e279c01-ce60-5c20-81e3-fd0d31451315",
          ":answer-field/key": "selection",
          ":answer-field/type": ":answer-field.type/radio",
