      ":question/id": "10612c5a-7835-56cd-9d52-f2a0bc2d944b",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/repairs/2026-10-19-checkbox-nonempty/baseline-samples/17592186054676/assets/1007ddb4c7597b49b86855e520a6e3b5f56be7c8c52188e90ab9ff9882344924.png)\n\nIdentify the points that lie on the line $y=3x+6$.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The plotted points correspond to the $xy$-pairs $A(-3,-3)$, $B(1,7)$, $C(0,6)$.\n\nTo check whether a point lies on the line, we substitute the coordinates of the point into the equation and determine whether the resulting statement is true.\n\n- Substituting the point $A(-3,-3)$ results in a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &= 3x+6 \\\\ (-3) &= 3(-3)+6 \\\\ -3 &= -3 && \\checkmark\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $A$ does lie on the line.\n\n- Substituting the point $B(1,7)$ results in a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &= 3x+6 \\\\ (7) &= 3(1)+6 \\\\ 7 &= 9 && \\times\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $B$ does not lie on the line.\n\n- Substituting the point $C(0,6)$ results in a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &= 3x+6 \\\\ (6) &= 3(0)+6 \\\\ 6 &= 6 && \\checkmark\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $C$ does lie on the line.\n\nFinally, only points $A$ and $C$ lie on the line.",
